#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Assert this setup's assumptions before every commit.
#
# The point of check.sh is that it runs without anyone remembering to run it —
# otherwise the repo's health depends on someone deciding to look, which is how
# it accumulated months of silent defects in the first place.
#
# This runs the FULL check.sh, machine half included, which is deliberate. The
# machine checks that can FAIL are all real breakage — a stow tree folded into a
# symlink, a pending stow, a dead git identity, a missing colour scheme or font,
# hooks not installed. Committing on top of any of those is how this repo
# accumulated months of silent defects.
#
# What makes that safe is that the volatile things are WARNINGS, not failures:
# a formatter missing from PATH reports `!` and does not fail the run. So a
# half-set-up machine can still commit. Asserted in tests/test-hooks.sh, because
# turning one of those into a hard failure would block every commit until
# someone installed a formatter, and the person it happened to would have no
# idea why.
#
# Bypass for a genuinely unrelated commit with: git commit --no-verify
exec "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/check.sh"
